529/529=4232\u221a6/529.\n\nThus, a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2=26450/529 -4232\u221a6/529 +721/529 +200\u221a6/529.\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(26450 +721)/529 + (-4232\u221a6 +200\u221a6)/529= (27171)/529 + (-4032\u221a6)/529.\n\nSimplify:\n\n27171 \u00f7529= 27171 \u00f7529. Let's compute 529*51=26979. 27171 -26979=192. 192 \u00f7529=192/529. So, 27171/529=51 +192/529.\n\nSimilarly, 4032 \u00f7529=4032 \u00f7529. 529*7=3703. 4032 -3703=329. So, 4032=529*7 +329. Thus, 4032/529=7 +329/529.\n\nThus, a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2=51 +192/529 - (7 +329/529)\u221a6.\n\nWait, no, actually:\n\nWait, a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2=27171/529 -4232\u221a6/529.\n\nWait, no, I think I made a miscalculation.\n\nWait, let me recompute:\n\na\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2=2*(25 -4\u221a6) + (25 +4\u221a6)^2 /23\u00b2\n\nCompute 2*(25 -4\u221a6)=50 -8\u221a6\n\nCompute (25 +4\u221a6)^2=625 + 200\u221a6 + 96=721 +200\u221a6\n\nThus, c\u00b2=(721 +200\u221a6)/529\n\nThus, a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2=50 -8\u221a6 + (721 +200\u221a6)/529\n\nConvert 50 to 50*529/529=26450/529\n\n-8\u221a6= (-8*529\u221a6)/529= (-4232\u221a6)/529\n\nThus, a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2=26450/529 -4232\u221a6/529 +721/529 +200\u221a6/529= (26450 +721)/529 + (-4232\u221a6 +200\u221a6)/529=27171/529 -4032\u221a6/529.\n\nSimplify:\n\n27171 \u00f7529: 529*51=26979, 27171 -26979=192, so 27171=529*51 +192. So, 27171/529=51 +192/529.\n\nSimilarly, 4032 \u00f7529: 529*7=3703, 4032 -3703=329, so 4032=529*7 +329, so 4032/529=7 +329/529.\n\nThus, a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2=51 +192/529 - (7 +329/529)\u221a6.\n\nWait, that can't be. Wait, the terms with \u221a6 are separate.\n\nWait, no, actually, in the numerator, it's 27171 -4032\u221a6, over 529.\n\nSo, a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2=(27171 -4032\u221a6)/529.\n\nWe can write this as (27171 -4032\u221a6)/529.\n\nSimplify numerator and denominator by GCD of 27171 and 4032 and 529.\n\nFirst, 529 is 23\u00b2, which is prime.\n\nCheck if 23 divides 27171: 27171 \u00f723= let's see, 23*1000=23000, 27171 -23000=4171. 23*180=4140, 4171 -4140=31. Not divisible.\n\nSimilarly, 4032 \u00f723: 23*175=4025, 4032-4025=7, so no.\n\nThus, 27171 and 4032 are not divisible by23.\n\nThus, the fraction is (27171 -4032\u221a6)/529.\n\nBut we can write this as:\n\n27171=23*1184.043\u2248 not integer. Wait, maybe factor 27171:\n\nDivide 27171 by 3: 2+7+1+7+1=18, divisible by 9. 27171 \u00f79=3019.\n\n3019: Let's see, 3019 \u00f77=431.285, not integer. 3019 \u00f713=232.23, nope. Maybe prime.\n\nSimilarly, 4032 is 2^6 *3^2 *7.\n\nSo, maybe this is the simplest form.\n\nThus, r\u00b2=(a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2)/4=(27171 -4032\u221a6)/(4*529)= (27171 -4032\u221a6)/2116.\n\nBut wait, r\u00b2 is equal to (a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2)/4, which is (27171 -4032\u221a6)/529 divided by 4, so (27171 -4032\u221a6)/(4*529)= (27171 -4032\u221a6)/2116.\n\nBut 27171 and 2116: 2116=46\u00b2=4*529. So, check if 27171 and 2116 have common factors.\n\n2116=4*529, and 27171: 27171 \u00f7529=27171/529\u224851.42. 529*51=26979, 27171-26979=192. So, 27171=529*51 +192, which is 529*51 +192. 192 and 529 are coprime because 192 is 2^6*3 and 529 is 23\u00b2. So, no common factors.\n\nSimilarly, 4032 and 2116: 2116=4*529. 4032 \u00f74=1008. 1008 and 529: 529 is prime, 1008=16*63, which is 16*7*9. 529 and 1008 are coprime. Thus, no common factors.\n\nThus, the fraction is reduced.\n\nThus, r\u00b2=(27171 -4032\u221a6)/2116.\n\nWait, but this seems messy. Maybe I made a mistake earlier.\n\nWait, perhaps instead of setting a=b, maybe the minimal sphere occurs when two edges are equal, but perhaps I should approach it differently.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use the inequality that relates the sum of squares, product, etc.\n\nGiven that for positive real numbers, (a +b +c)^2 >=3(ab +bc +ca). But not sure.\n\nAlternatively, use the method of Lagrange multipliers.\n\nLet me set up the Lagrangian again.\n\nDefine f(a,b,c)=a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2\n\nSubject to g(a,b,c)=2(ab + bc + ca)=54.\n\nWait, no, in the problem, surface area is 54, so 2(ab + bc + ca)=54, so ab + bc + ca=27.\n\nWait, the initial problem says surface area 54, so 2(ab + bc + ca)=54, so ab + bc + ca=27.\n\nAnd volume=abc=23.\n\nSo, constraints: ab + bc + ca=27, abc=23.\n\nWe need to minimize a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2.\n\nSo, actually, the Lagrangian is:\n\nL= a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2 -\u03bb(ab + bc + ca -27) -\u03bc(abc -23)\n\nCompute partial derivatives:\n\n\u2202L/\u2202a=2a -\u03bb(b +c) -\u03bc(bc)=0\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\u2202L/\u2202b=2b -\u03bb(a +c) -\u03bc(ac)=0\n\n\u2202L/\u2202c=2c -\u03bb(a +b) -\u03bc(ab)=0\n\nAnd constraints:\n\nab + bc + ca=27\n\nabc=23\n\nThis system is symmetric in a, b, c, so likely the minimal occurs when two variables are equal.\n\nAssume a=b.\n\nThen, from constraints:\n\nab + bc + ca= a\u00b2 +2ac=27\n\nabc= a\u00b2c=23\n\nFrom abc=23, a\u00b2c=23, so c=23/(a\u00b2)\n\nSubstitute into ab + bc + ca= a\u00b2 +2ac=27:\n\na\u00b2 +2a*(23/(a\u00b2))=27 \u21d2a\u00b2 +46/a=27\n\nMultiply both sides by a:\n\na\u00b3 +46=27a \u21d2a\u00b3 -27a +46=0\n\nFind real roots of a\u00b3 -27a +46=0.\n\nTry a=3: 27 -81 +46= -8\u22600\n\na=4:64 -108 +46=2\u22600\n\na=2:8 -54 +46=0! Yes, a=2 is a root.\n\nThus, factor (a -2)(a\u00b2 +2a -23)=0\n\nThus, a=2 or a\u00b2 +2a -23=0.\n\nThus, a=2 or a=(-2 \u00b1\u221a(4 +92))/2=(-2 \u00b1\u221a96)/2=(-2 \u00b14\u221a6)/2=-1 \u00b12\u221a6.\n\nSince a>0, a=2 or a= -1 +2\u221a6\u22483.898.\n\nCase1: a=2\n\nThen, c=23/(a\u00b2)=23/4=5.75.\n\nThus, a=b=2, c=23/4.\n\nCompute a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2=4 +4 + (23/4)^2=8 +529/16=8 +33.0625=41.0625=657/16.\n\nCase2: a= -1 +2\u221a6.\n\nCompute c=23/(a\u00b2)=23/( ( -1 +2\u221a6 )\u00b2 )=23/(1 -4\u221a6 +24)=23/(25 -4\u221a6 )\n\nRationalize denominator:\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by (25 +4\u221a6 ):\n\nc=23*(25 +4\u221a6 )/(25\u00b2 - (4\u221a6 )\u00b2 )=23*(25 +4\u221a6 )/(625 - 96)=23*(25 +4\u221a6 )/529= (25 +4\u221a6 )/23.\n\nThus, c=(25 +4\u221a6 )/23.\n\nCompute a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2: Since a=b= -1 +2\u221a6, c=(25 +4\u221a6 )/23.\n\nCompute a\u00b2= ( -1 +2\u221a6 )\u00b2=1 -4\u221a6 +24=25 -4\u221a6.\n\nThus, a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2=2*(25 -4\u221a6 ) + (25 +4\u221a6 )\u00b2 /529.\n\nCompute numerator:\n\n2*(25 -4\u221a6 )=50 -8\u221a6\n\n(25 +4\u221a6 )\u00b2=625 +200\u221a6 +96=721 +200\u221a6\n\nThus, a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2=50 -8\u221a6 + (721 +200\u221a6 )/529.\n\nConvert 50 to 50*529/529=26450/529.\n\n-8\u221a6= (-8*529\u221a6 )/529= -4232\u221a6 /529.\n\nThus, a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2=26450/529 -4232\u221a6 /529 +721/529 +200\u221a6 /529= (26450 +721)/529 + (-4232\u221a6 +200\u221a6 )/529=27171/529 -4032\u221a6 /529.\n\nThus, a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2=(27171 -4032\u221a6 )/529.\n\nTherefore, r\u00b2=(a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2)/4=(27171 -4032\u221a6 )/(4*529 )= (27171 -4032\u221a6 )/2116.\n\nWait, 2116=46\u00b2=4*529.\n\nBut 27171 and 2116: 27171 \u00f72116\u224812.84, but not integer. Check GCD(27171,2116).\n\n2116=4*529=4*23\u00b2.\n\n27171 \u00f723=27171/23\u22481183.956, not integer. So, no common factors.\n\nThus, the fraction is reduced.\n\nThus, r\u00b2=(27171 -4032\u221a6 )/2116.\n\nBut the problem states that r\u00b2 can be written as p/q where p and q are coprime positive integers.\n\nThus, p=27171 -4032\u221a6 and q=2116? But that can't be because \u221a6 is irrational. So, maybe I made a mistake.\n\nWait, no, r\u00b2 is (a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2)/4, which is (27171 -4032\u221a6 )/(4*529 )= (27171 -4032\u221a6 )/2116.\n\nWait, but that still has \u221a6, which is irrational. So, that can't be expressed as a rational number. So, I must have messed up.\n\nWait, wait, no, because in the minimal case, when a= -1 +2\u221a6, which is approximately3.898, then c=(25 +4\u221a6 )/23\u2248(25 +9.798)/23\u224834.798/23\u22481.513.\n\nThen, a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2\u2248(3.898)^2 + (3.898)^2 + (1.513)^2\u224815.19 +15.19 +2.29\u224832.67.\n\nBut 32.67 is roughly 32.67=32 + 2/3\u224897/3. Hmm, but 97/3\u224832.333. Close but not exact.\n\nWait, maybe it's rational. Wait, 27171 -4032\u221a6 over2116 is the expression. But that can't be rational. Hmm.\n\nWait, perhaps my earlier approach is wrong. Maybe I need to find the minimal sphere that can contain any box with surface area 54 and volume23.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the minimal sphere is achieved when the box is a cube.\n\nWait, but surface area=54. If it's a cube, then 6s\u00b2=54 \u21d2s\u00b2=9\u21d2s=3. Volume would be 27, which is more than23. So, cube is not possible.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the minimal sphere is achieved when the box is as \"cube-like\" as possible.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using the inequality that relates the space diagonal to the surface area and volume.\n\nBut I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe think of the box as having sides x, y, z.\n\nGiven 2(xy + yz + zx)=54 \u21d2xy + yz + zx=27.\n\nAnd xyz=23.\n\nWe need to minimize (x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2)/4, since r=(sqrt(x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2))/2.\n\nSo, need to minimize x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2.\n\nGiven the constraints.\n\nI think this is the same as what I did earlier.\n\nBut getting a square root in the expression complicates things.\n\nWait, perhaps use Cauchy-Schwarz.\n\nWe have (x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2)(1 +1 +1) >= (x + y + z)^2.\n\nBut not sure if helpful.\n\nAlternatively, use AM \u2265 GM.\n\nBut maybe use the identity that (x + y + z)^2 =x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2 + 2(xy + yz + zx).\n\nThus, x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2=(x + y + z)^2 -2(xy + yz + zx)= (x + y + z)^2 -54.\n\nSo, to minimize x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2, we need to minimize (x + y + z)^2.\n\nBut how?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps express x + y + z in terms of the constraints.\n\nBut we know that xyz=23, which is fixed.\n\nSo, perhaps using AM \u2265 GM for x, y, z.\n\n(x + y + z)/3 >= (xyz)^(1/3)=23^(1/3)\u22482.84.\n\nSo, x + y + z >=3*2.84\u22488.52.\n\nThus, (x + y + z)^2 >=72.6.\n\nThus, x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2= (x + y + z)^2 -54 >=72.6 -54=18.6.\n\nBut 18.6=93/5.\n\nBut this is just a lower bound, and the minimal might be higher.\n\nBut in reality, when does equality hold?\n\nEquality in AM \u2265 GM when x=y=z. But in our case, x=y=z gives a cube with surface area54, which has volume27\u226023. So, the minimal sphere is achieved when the box is not a cube.\n\nThus, perhaps the minimal x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 + z\u00b2 is around 32.67.\n\nBut the question says that r\u00b2 can be written as p/q. So, that suggests that r\u00b2 is rational.\n\nBut according to our earlier calculation, r\u00b2=(27171 -4032\u221a6 )/2116, which is irrational.\n\nThus, I must have made a miscalculation.\n\nWait, let's recast the problem.\n\nLet me denote a, b, c as the sides.\n\nGiven 2(ab + bc + ca)=54 \u21d2ab + bc + ca=27.\n\nAnd abc=23.\n\nWe need to find the minimal r such that r is the radius of the smallest sphere containing the box, which is equivalent to r= (1/2)sqrt(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 + c\u00b2).\n\nThus, r\u00b2=(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 + c\u00b2)/4.\n\nSo, need to find the minimal value of (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 + c\u00b2)/4, given ab + bc + ca=27 and abc=23.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use Lagrange multipliers.\n\nDefine f(a,b,c)=a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 + c\u00b2.\n\nSubject to g(a,b,c)=ab + bc + ca=27 and h(a,b,c)=abc=23.\n\nWait, but this is a problem with two constraints. Maybe use substitution.\n\nFrom h(a,b,c)=23, express c=23/(ab).\n\nSubstitute into g(a,b,c)=ab + b*(23/(ab)) + a*(23/(ab))=ab +23/a +23/b=27.\n\nThus, ab +23/a +23/b=27.\n\nLet\u2019s set a=b for symmetry, so 2a +23/a=27.\n\nWait, but if a=b, then c=23/(a\u00b2).\n\nThus, 2a +23/a=27.\n\nMultiply both sides by a: 2a\u00b2 +23=27a \u21d22a\u00b2 -27a +23=0.\n\nSolutions: a=(27\u00b1sqrt(729 -184))/4=(27\u00b1sqrt(545))/4.\n\nsqrt(545)\u224823.34.\n\nThus, a=(27 +23.34)/4\u224850.34/4\u224812.585 or a=(27 -23.34)/4\u22483.66/4\u22480.915.\n\nBut a must be positive. So, a\u224812.585 or\u22480.915.\n\nCompute a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 +c\u00b2: if a\u224812.585, then b\u224812.585, c=23/(a\u00b2)\u224823/161\u22480.142.\n\nThus, a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2\u22482*(161.8)+0.02\u2248323.6 +0.02\u2248323.62.\n\nIf a\u22480.915, then c=23/(0.915\u00b2)=23/0.837\u224827.57.\n\nThus, a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2\u22482*(0.837)+27.57\u22481.674 +27.57\u224829.244.\n\nWait, but earlier when I set two variables equal, I got a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2\u224832.67, which is between these two.\n\nWait, perhaps the minimal is\u224829.24.\n\nBut again, getting an approximate value.\n\nWait, maybe using AM-QM inequality.\n\nBut not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use the method of Lagrange multipliers with two constraints.\n\nBut since it's complicated, maybe think about the minimal occurs when a, b, c are equal, but that's not possible because abc=23, which is not a cube.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps set one variable to be minimal, like c minimal.\n\nBut not sure.\n\nAlternatively, let me think about the problem differently.\n\nGiven that ab + bc + ca=27 and abc=23.\n\nLet me denote p=a + b + c, q=ab + bc + ca=27, r=abc=23.\n\nBut Newton's identities relate the sums of powers.\n\nBut perhaps not helpful here.\n\nAlternatively, consider that for three variables, the expression a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 +c\u00b2 can be written as (a + b + c)^2 -2(ab + bc + ca)=p\u00b2 -54.\n\nThus, to minimize a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 +c\u00b2, we need to minimize p\u00b2, where p=a + b + c.\n\nGiven that ab + bc + ca=27 and abc=23.\n\nWe can also recall that for three positive real numbers, by AM \u2265 GM:\n\n(a + b + c)/3 \u2265 (abc)^(1/3)=23^(1/3)\u22482.84.\n\nThus, p\u22653*2.84\u22488.52.\n\nThus, p\u00b2\u2265(8.52)^2\u224872.6.\n\nThus, a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 +c\u00b2=p\u00b2 -54\u226572.6 -54\u224818.6.\n\nBut 18.6 is a lower bound, but the actual minimal may be higher.\n\nBut how to find the minimal p given that p=a + b + c and ab + bc + ca=27, abc=23.\n\nAlternatively, use the method of Lagrange multipliers for two constraints.\n\nDefine L=a\u00b2 +b\u00b2 +c\u00b2 -\u03bb(ab + bc + ca -27) -\u03bc(abc -23).\n\nTake partial derivatives:\n\n\u2202L/\u2202a=2a -\u03bb(b + c) -\u03bc bc=0\n\nSimilarly,\n\n\u2202L/\u2202b=2b -\u03bb(a + c) -\u03bc ac=0\n\n\u2202L/\u2202c=2c -\u03bb(a + b) -\u03bc ab=0\n\nAnd constraints:\n\nab + bc + ca=27\n\nabc=23\n\nAssuming that two variables are equal, say a=b.\n\nThen, from the first two equations:\n\n2a -\u03bb(a + c) -\u03bc a c=0\n\nSimilarly, 2a -\u03bb(a + c) -\u03bc a c=0\n\nFrom the third equation:\n\n2c -\u03bb(2a) -\u03bc a\u00b2=0\n\nFrom the first equation: 2a -\u03bb(a + c) -\u03bc a c=0 \u21d22a=\u03bb(a + c) +\u03bc a c.\n\nFrom third equation: 2c=2\u03bb a +\u03bc a\u00b2.\n\nLet me denote \u03bb= (2a -2c)/a from third equation.\n\nWait, no.\n\nFrom third equation: 2c=2\u03bb a +\u03bc a\u00b2 \u21d2\u03bb=(2c -\u03bc a\u00b2)/(2a)\n\nSimilarly, from first equation: 2a=\u03bb(a + c) +\u03bc a c.\n\nSubstitute \u03bb:\n\n2a= [(2c -\u03bc a\u00b2)/(2a)]*(a + c) +\u03bc a c.\n\nMultiply through:\n\n2a= [ (2c - \u03bc a\u00b2)(a + c) ]/(2a) + \u03bc a c.\n\nMultiply both sides by 2a:\n\n4a\u00b2= (2c - \u03bc a\u00b2)(a + c) + 2 \u03bc a\u00b2 c.\n\nExpand RHS:\n\n(2c)(a + c) - \u03bc a\u00b2(a + c) + 2 \u03bc a\u00b2 c.\n\n= 2ac + 2c\u00b2 - \u03bc a\u00b3 - \u03bc a\u00b2 c + 2 \u03bc a\u00b2 c.\n\n= 2ac + 2c\u00b2 - \u03bc a\u00b3 + \u03bc a\u00b2 c.\n\nThus, 4a\u00b2=2ac + 2c\u00b2 - \u03bc a\u00b3 + \u03bc a\u00b2 c.\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\n4a\u00b2 -2ac -2c\u00b2 + \u03bc a\u00b3 - \u03bc a\u00b2 c=0.\n\nFactor \u03bc:\n\n4a\u00b2 -2ac -2c\u00b2 + \u03bc a\u00b2(a -c)=0.\n\nNow, from constraint ab + bc + ca=27, since a=b, we have a\u00b2 +2ac=27.\n\nFrom abc=23, a\u00b2 c=23.\n\nThus, c=23/(a\u00b2).\n\nSubstitute c=23/(a\u00b2) into equation above.\n\nCompute 4a\u00b2 -2ac -2c\u00b2 + \u03bc a\u00b2(a -c)=0.\n\nFirst, compute each term:\n\n4a\u00b2: 4a\u00b2\n\n-2ac: -2a*(23/a\u00b2)= -46/a\n\n-2c\u00b2: -2*(23/a\u00b2)\u00b2= -2*(529)/a\u2074= -1058/a\u2074\n\n+\u03bc a\u00b2(a -c): \u03bc a\u00b2(a -23/a\u00b2)= \u03bc(a\u00b3 -23/a)\n\nSo, equation becomes:\n\n4a\u00b2 -46/a -1058/a\u2074 + \u03bc(a\u00b3 -23/a)=0.\n\nNow, from 4a\u00b2 -46/a -1058/a\u2074 + \u03bc(a\u00b3 -23/a)=0.\n\nAlso, from c=23/a\u00b2, we can express \u03bc in terms of a.\n\nWait, but also from 4a\u00b2 -2ac -2c\u00b2=0? Wait, no, we have the above equation.\n\nWait, perhaps we can express \u03bc from this equation.\n\nLet me write the equation as:\n\n4a\u00b2 -46/a -1058/a\u2074 = -\u03bc(a\u00b3 -23/a).\n\nSo,\n\n\u03bc = [ -4a\u00b2 +46/a +1058/a\u2074 ] / (a\u00b3 -23/a )\n\nSimplify numerator:\n\n-4a\u00b2 +46/a +1058/a\u2074= (-4a^6 +46a^3 +1058)/a^4.\n\nDenominator:\n\na\u00b3 -23/a= (a^4 -23)/a.\n\nThus,\n\n\u03bc= [ (-4a^6 +46a^3 +1058)/a^4 ] / [ (a^4 -23)/a ]\n\n= [ (-4a^6 +46a^3 +1058)/a^4 ] * [ a/(a^4 -23) ]\n\n= [ (-4a^6 +46a^3 +1058) * a ] / [ a^4(a^4 -23) ]\n\n= [ (-4a^7 +46a^4 +1058a ) ] / [ a^8 -23a^4 ]\n\nBut this seems getting too complicated.\n\nPerhaps instead, let me use the other equation.\n\nWait, from c=23/a\u00b2, we can write c=23/a\u00b2, so c=23/a\u00b2.\n\nFrom ab + bc + ca=27, since a=b, so a\u00b2 +2ac=27.\n\nThus, a\u00b2 +2a*(23/a\u00b2)=27 \u21d2a\u00b2 +46/a=27.\n\nMultiply both sides by a:\n\na\u00b3 +46=27a \u21d2a\u00b3 -27a +46=0.\n\nSo, we have a cubic equation: a\u00b3 -27a +46=0.\n\nWe can try to find rational roots using Rational Root Theorem.\n\nPossible roots are factors of 46 over 1: \u00b11, \u00b12, \u00b123, \u00b146.\n\nTest a=1: 1 -27 +46=20\u22600.\n\na=2:8 -54 +46=0. So, a=2 is a root.\n\nThus, factor as (a -2)(a\u00b2 +2a -23)=0.\n\nThus, a=2, or a=(-2 \u00b1\u221a(4 +92))/2= (-2 \u00b1\u221a96)/2= (-2 \u00b14\u221a6)/2= -1 \u00b12\u221a6.\n\nSince a>0, a=2 or a= -1 +2\u221a6\u22483.898.\n\nThus, a=2 or a= -1 +2\u221a6.\n\nSo, case 1: a=2.\n\nThen, c=23/(2\u00b2)=23/4=5.75.\n\nThus, dimensions: a=b=2, c=5.75.\n\nCompute space diagonal: sqrt(2\u00b2 +2\u00b2 +5.75\u00b2)=sqrt(4 +4 +33.0625)=sqrt(41.0625)=6.408...\n\nBut 41.0625= 657/16.\n\nWait, 41.0625=41 +1/16= (41*16 +1)/16=656 +1=657/16. So sqrt(657/16)=sqrt(657)/4.\n\nBut 657=9*73, so sqrt(657)=3*sqrt(73).\n\nThus, space diagonal is 3\u221a73 /4.\n\nThus, radius r= (3\u221a73)/8.\n\nThus, r\u00b2=(9*73)/64=657/64.\n\nCase 2: a= -1 +2\u221a6\u22483.898.\n\nThen, c=23/(a\u00b2).\n\nCompute a\u00b2: (-1 +2\u221a6)\u00b2=1 -4\u221a6 +24=25 -4\u221a6.\n\nThus, c=23/(25 -4\u221a6).\n\nMultiply numerator and denominator by (25 +4\u221a6):\n\nc=23*(25 +4\u221a6)/[(25)^2 - (4\u221a6)^2]=23*(25 +4\u221a6)/(625 - 96)=23*(25 +4\u221a6)/529= (25 +4\u221a6)/23.\n\nThus, c=(25 +4\u221a6)/23.\n\nThus, dimensions: a=b= -1 +2\u221a6, c=(25 +4\u221a6)/23.\n\nCompute space diagonal: sqrt(a\u00b2 +a\u00b2 +c\u00b2).\n\nCompute a\u00b2=25 -4\u221a6, so 2a\u00b2=50 -4\u221a6.\n\nc\u00b2= (25 +4\u221a6)^2 /23\u00b2= (625 +200\u221a6 +96)/529= (721 +200\u221a6)/529.\n\nThus, space diagonal squared is 2a\u00b2 +c\u00b2= (50 -4\u221a6) + (721 +200\u221a6)/529.\n\nWait, maybe better to compute:\n\nSpace diagonal squared= 2*(25 -4\u221a6) + (25 +4\u221a6)^2 /23\u00b2.\n\nWait, 2*(25 -4\u221a6)=50 -8\u221a6.\n\n(25 +4\u221a6)^2=625 +200\u221a6 +96=721 +200\u221a6.\n\nThus, space diagonal squared=50 -8\u221a6 + (721 +200\u221a6)/529.\n\nConvert 50 to over 529: 50=50*529/529=26450/529.\n\nSimilarly, -8\u221a6= (-8\u221a6 *529)/529= (-4232\u221a6)/529.\n\nThus, space diagonal squared= [26450 -4232\u221a6 +721 +200\u221a6]/529= (26450 +721)/529 + (-4232\u221a6 +200\u221a6)/529.\n\nCompute 26450 +721=27171.\n\nAnd -4232\u221a6 +200\u221a6= -4032\u221a6.\n\nThus, space diagonal squared=27171 -4032\u221a6 all over 529.\n\nThus, r\u00b2= (27171 -4032\u221a6)/529.\n\nWait, but we need to rationalize this or see if it's reducible.\n\nWait, 27171 and 4032: let's check GCD(27171,4032).\n\nCompute GCD(4032,27171).\n\n27171 \u00f74032=6 with remainder 27171 -6*4032=27171-24192=2979.\n\nGCD(4032,2979).\n\n4032 \u00f72979=1 with remainder 4032-2979=1053.\n\nGCD(2979,1053).\n\n2979 \u00f71053=2 with remainder 2979-2*1053=2979-2106=873.\n\nGCD(1053,873).\n\n1053 \u00f7873=1 with remainder 180.\n\nGCD(873,180).\n\n873 \u00f7180=4 with remainder 93.\n\nGCD(180,93).\n\n180 \u00f793=1 with remainder 87.\n\nGCD(93,87).\n\n93 \u00f787=1 with remainder 6.\n\nGCD(87,6).\n\n87 \u00f76=14 with remainder 3.\n\nGCD(6,3)=3.\n\nThus, GCD is 3.\n\nSimilarly, 4032 \u00f73=1344, 529 is prime? Let me check.\n\n529: 23\u00b2=529, yes.\n\nThus, 529=23\u00b2.\n\nThus, 27171=3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  3*23*  