D is the entire population, |A \u222a B \u222a C \u222a D| = 900.\n\nSo, substituting into the formula:\n\n900 = 195 + 367 + 562 + 900 - (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 D| + |B \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 D| + |C \u2229 D|) + (|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 D| + |A \u2229 C \u2229 D| + |B \u2229 C \u2229 D|) - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|\n\nHmm, that seems complicated, but maybe we can find some relations.\n\nWait, another approach. Since all residents own candy hearts, then the intersections involving D will be the same as the intersections of A, B, C, because D is everyone. So, for example, |A \u2229 D| is just |A|, since everyone in A also owns candy hearts. Similarly, |B \u2229 D| = |B|, |C \u2229 D| = |C|, and |A \u2229 B \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 B|, because all of them own candy hearts. Similarly, |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D| is just |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|, since all of them are in D.\n\nSo, maybe we can substitute some of these terms.\n\nLet me denote:\n\n- Let x = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| (since D is everyone)\n- Let y = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| (this is the number of people who own A, B, C, but not necessarily D, but in reality, since D is everyone, y is the same as x)\nWait, no, that might not be correct. Wait, if someone is in A \u2229 B \u2229 C, then they are in D as well, since everyone is in D. So, actually, the number of people who own exactly three things would be |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|, but since they also own D, they own all four. Wait, no, not exactly.\n\nWait, hold on. The problem says 234 residents own exactly three of these things. So, that means they own three things but not the fourth. But since D is candy hearts, which everyone owns, so if someone owns exactly three things, they must own three among A, B, C, and not the fourth? But wait, since D is a given, everyone owns D, so actually, the number of people who own exactly three things would be |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|. But wait, no, that doesn't make sense.\n\nWait, hold on. Maybe I need to think differently.\n\nLet me consider that D is a set that everyone is in. So, for any intersection involving D, it's just the same as the intersection without D. For example, |A \u2229 D| = |A|, since everyone in A is in D.\n\nSimilarly, |A \u2229 B \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 B|, because all of them are in D.\n\nTherefore, when considering the inclusion-exclusion principle, the terms like |A \u2229 D|, |B \u2229 D|, etc., can be replaced by |A|, |B|, |C|.\n\nSimilarly, the triple intersections involving D can be replaced by the intersections of A, B, C.\n\nSo, let's rewrite the inclusion-exclusion formula:\n\n900 = |A| + |B| + |C| + |D| - (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 D| + |B \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 D| + |C \u2229 D|) + (|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 D| + |A \u2229 C \u2229 D| + |B \u2229 C \u2229 D|) - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|\n\nBut substituting |A \u2229 D| = |A|, |B \u2229 D| = |B|, |C \u2229 D| = |C|, |A \u2229 B \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 B|, |A \u2229 C \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 C|, |B \u2229 C \u2229 D| = |B \u2229 C|, and |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nSo, substituting these into the equation:\n\n900 = 195 + 367 + 562 + 900 - (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |A| + |B \u2229 C| + |B| + |C|) + (|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|\n\nHmm, that's a bit messy, but let's note that |A|, |B|, |C|, |D| are given, so we can substitute their values:\n\n900 = 195 + 367 + 562 + 900 - (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + 195 + |B \u2229 C| + 367 + 562) + (|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|\n\nWait, that's confusing. Maybe I need to reorganize the equation step by step.\n\nFirst, let's compute |A| + |B| + |C| + |D|:\n\n195 + 367 + 562 + 900 = 2024.\n\nSo, 900 = 2024 - (sum of all pairwise intersections) + (sum of all triple intersections) - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|\n\nBut to express the sum of all pairwise intersections, we have:\n\nsum of all pairwise intersections = |A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 D| + |B \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 D| + |C \u2229 D|\n\nBut as established earlier, |A \u2229 D| = |A| = 195, |B \u2229 D| = |B| = 367, |C \u2229 D| = |C| = 562.\n\nTherefore, sum of all pairwise intersections = |A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + 195 + |B \u2229 C| + 367 + 562\n\nSimilarly, the sum of all triple intersections:\n\nsum of all triple intersections = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 D| + |A \u2229 C \u2229 D| + |B \u2229 C \u2229 D|\n\nAgain, substituting:\n\n|A \u2229 B \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 B|, |A \u2229 C \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 C|, |B \u2229 C \u2229 D| = |B \u2229 C|\n\nSo, sum of all triple intersections = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|\n\nSo, putting it all back into the inclusion-exclusion formula:\n\n900 = 2024 - [ |A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + 195 + |B \u2229 C| + 367 + 562 ] + [ |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C| ] - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|\n\nHmm, let's denote some variables to make this manageable.\n\nLet me let:\n\n- Let p = |A \u2229 B|\n- Let q = |A \u2229 C|\n- Let r = |B \u2229 C|\n\nSo, substituting:\n\nsum of pairwise intersections = p + q + 195 + r + 367 + 562 = p + q + r + (195 + 367 + 562) = p + q + r + 1124\n\nSimilarly, sum of triple intersections = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + p + q + r\n\nLet me denote:\n\n- Let s = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|\n\nSo, sum of triple intersections = s + p + q + r\n\nPutting back into the equation:\n\n900 = 2024 - [p + q + r + 1124] + [s + p + q + r] - s\n\nLet me compute this step by step.\n\nFirst, 2024 - (p + q + r + 1124) = 2024 - 1124 - p - q - r = 900 - p - q - r\n\nThen, adding [s + p + q + r] gives:\n\n900 - p - q - r + s + p + q + r = 900 + s\n\nThen, subtracting s:\n\n900 + s - s = 900\n\nSo, 900 = 900\n\nWait, that's an identity. It doesn't help us find s. Hmm, that's unexpected.\n\nSo, it seems that the inclusion-exclusion principle leads to an identity, which doesn't help us find the value of s, which is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|, or equivalently, |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|, since D is everyone.\n\nSo, maybe we need another approach.\n\nLet me think about the number of people who own exactly two or three things.\n\nGiven that:\n\n- 437 residents own exactly two of the things (A, B, C, D). Since D is everyone, owning exactly two things would mean owning two of A, B, C, and not owning the third. So, the number of people owning exactly two is equal to the number of people in each pairwise intersection minus those who are in all three.\n\nSimilarly, 234 residents own exactly three things. But wait, since D is everyone, does that mean they own three of A, B, C, D? But since D is everyone, if someone owns three of A, B, C, they must own three and the fourth is D, which they already own. So, perhaps the number of people owning exactly three things is equal to the number of people in the triple intersections minus those who are in all four.\n\nWait, maybe it's better to use the principle of inclusion-exclusion considering only A, B, C, since D is the entire set.\n\nSo, let me denote:\n\n- Let a = |A| = 195\n- Let b = |B| = 367\n- Let c = |C| = 562\n- Let d = |D| = 900\n\nWe are to find |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|, which is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|, since D is everyone.\n\nLet me denote:\n\n- Let x be the number of people who own exactly two of A, B, C (and not all three)\n- Let y be the number of people who own exactly three of A, B, C (and not all four)\n- Let z be the number of people who own all four.\n\nWait, but D is everyone, so actually, the number of people who own exactly three of A, B, C is the number of people who own three of A, B, C, and not the fourth? But since D is everyone, if you own three of A, B, C, you automatically own D. So, actually, the number of people owning exactly three of A, B, C is equal to the number of people who own exactly three of A, B, C, and since D is everyone, those people also own D. So, the count is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| minus those who own all four.\n\nBut the problem states that 234 residents own exactly three of these things. So, does that mean they own exactly three, not four? So, it's |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - z = 234.\n\nSimilarly, 437 residents own exactly two of these things. So, that would be the number of people in each pairwise intersection minus those who are in all three.\n\nSo, the formula for the number of people owning exactly two of A, B, C is:\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) - 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + z - x = 437\n\nWait, maybe I need to think more carefully.\n\nLet me recall that in inclusion-exclusion for three sets, the number of people owning exactly two is:\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) - 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|\n\nBut since each pairwise intersection includes those who are in all three, we subtract 3 times the triple intersection to avoid double-counting.\n\nBut in our case, we have an additional z, which is the number of people owning all four. Wait, maybe not. Let me think.\n\nWait, actually, in our case, since D is everyone, the number of people who own exactly two of A, B, C is equal to the number of people who own exactly two of A, B, C and not D. But since D is everyone, this is zero. So, actually, the number of people owning exactly two of A, B, C is equal to the number of people owning exactly two of A, B, C, and not owning the third.\n\nWait, perhaps I'm getting confused.\n\nWait, maybe let's consider that D is everyone, so any resident is automatically in D. So, if someone owns exactly two of A, B, C, they must own two of A, B, C and not the third, and since they are in D, they are in the intersection of two sets but not the third. So, the number of people owning exactly two is equal to (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C| - 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|).\n\nSimilarly, the number of people owning