 \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) - 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nSimilarly, the number of people owning exactly three is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|, but since D is everyone, |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D| is just |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|, so the number of people owning exactly three is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - z = 234.\n\nWait, so if |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - z = 234, then z = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - 234.\n\nBut we don't know |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| yet.\n\nSimilarly, the number of people owning exactly two is (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) - 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = 437.\n\nSo, if I can find |A \u2229 B|, |A \u2229 C|, |B \u2229 C|, and |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|, I can find z.\n\nBut I don't know |A \u2229 B|, |A \u2229 C|, |B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, but maybe I can find these using the inclusion-exclusion principle.\n\nWe know that:\n\n|A \u222a B \u222a C \u222a D| = |A| + |B| + |C| + |D| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| - |A \u2229 D| - |B \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 D| - |C \u2229 D| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 D| + |A \u2229 C \u2229 D| + |B \u2229 C \u2229 D| - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D|.\n\nBut again, D is everyone, so |A \u2229 D| = |A|, |B \u2229 D| = |B|, |C \u2229 D| = |C|, |A \u2229 B \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 B|, |A \u2229 C \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 C|, |B \u2229 C \u2229 D| = |B \u2229 C|, and |A \u2229 B \u2229 C \u2229 D| = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nSo, substituting:\n\n900 = 195 + 367 + 562 - (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |A| + |B \u2229 C| + |B| + |C|) + (|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, that seems complicated. Let me write it step by step.\n\nFirst, the formula:\n\n900 = |A| + |B| + |C| - (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + (|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, hold on, perhaps I need to think differently.\n\nWait, maybe it's