s own at least one item (candy hearts). So, the breakdown is:\n\n- 0 additional items: 0, because everyone owns candy hearts.\n\nWait, no, wait: no, sorry, perhaps not. Let me clarify.\n\nEach resident owns candy hearts, so the ownership of A, B, C is in addition. So, the possible counts are:\n\n- 0: own only candy hearts.\n\n- 1: own candy hearts and exactly one of A, B, C.\n\n- 2: own candy hearts and exactly two of A, B, C.\n\n- 3: own candy hearts and exactly three of A, B, C.\n\n- 4: own candy hearts and all four.\n\nTherefore, the total number of residents is:\n\nNumber owning exactly 0 additional items: 0, since everyone owns candy hearts.\n\nNumber owning exactly 1 additional item: y.\n\nNumber owning exactly 2 additional items: 437.\n\nNumber owning exactly 3 additional items: 234.\n\nNumber owning exactly 4 additional items: z (unknown).\n\nSo, total residents: 0 + y + 437 + 234 + z = 900.\n\nThus, y + z = 900 - 437 - 234 = 900 - 671 = 229.\n\nSo, y + z = 229.\n\nBut y is the number of residents owning exactly 1 additional item (A, B, or C), so y = |A| + |B| + |C| - 2|A \u2229 B| - 2|A \u2229 C| - 2|B \u2229 C| + 4|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, no. Let me think about the formula for the number of residents owning exactly one additional item.\n\nThe number of residents owning exactly one of A, B, C is:\n\n(|A| + |B| + |C| - 3|A \u2229 B| - 3|A \u2229 C| - 3|B \u2229 C| + 6|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|)/2.\n\nWait, perhaps not. Let me recall.\n\nIn inclusion-exclusion, the number of elements in exactly one set is:\n\n(|A| + |B| + |C| - 3|A \u2229 B| - 3|A \u2229 C| - 3|B \u2229 C| + 6|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|).\n\nWait, no, actually, the formula for exactly one is:\n\n(|A| + |B| + |C| - 3|A \u2229 B| - 3|A \u2229 C| - 3|B \u2229 C| + 6|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|) divided by something.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to recall the formula.\n\nIn general, for exactly k sets in inclusion-exclusion, the formula is:\n\nC(k) = \u03a3|A_i| - \u03a3|A_i \u2229 A_j| + \u03a3|A_i \u2229 A_j \u2229 A_k| - ... + (-1)^{m+1}|A_1 \u2229 ... \u2229 A_m}|.\n\nBut for exactly one, it's:\n\nC(1) = |A| + |B| + |C| - 3|A \u2229 B| - 3|A \u2229 C| - 3|B \u2229 C| + 6|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, not sure. Maybe it's better to refer to the inclusion-exclusion principle.\n\nWait, let me think differently.\n\nThe number of residents owning exactly one of A, B, C is equal to the sum of the residents owning only A, only B, only C.\n\nWhich can be expressed as:\n\n(|A| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|) + (|B| - |A \u2229 B| - |B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|) + (|C| - |A \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|).\n\nSo, adding these up, we get:\n\n(|A| + |B| + |C|) - 2(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nTherefore, y = |A| + |B| + |C| - 2(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut we know that y + z = 229, and from equation (1), we have:\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) - |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = 224.\n\nLet me denote S = |A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|, and T = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nSo, from equation (1), S - T = 224.\n\nWe also have y = |A| + |B| + |C| - 2S + 3T = 229 - z.\n\nBut we need another equation. Let's see.\n\nWe also know that z is the number of residents owning all four, so z = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = T.\n\nWait, no, z is the number of residents owning all four, which would be |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|, which is T. So z = T.\n\nTherefore, we have y + z = 229, so y = 229 - z.\n\nBut y = |A| + |B| + |C| - 2S + 3T.\n\nSo, 229 - z = |A| + |B| + |C| - 2S + 3T.\n\nWe need to express this in terms of known quantities.\n\nWe know that |A| = 195, |B| = 367, |C| = 562.\n\nSo, 229 - z = 195 + 367 + 562 - 2S + 3T.\n\nCompute 195 + 367 + 562.\n\n195 + 367 = 562, 562 + 562 = 1124.\n\nSo, 229 - z = 1124 - 2S + 3T.\n\nBut from equation (1), S = 224 + T.\n\nSo, substitute S = 224 + T.\n\nThen,\n\n229 - z = 1124 - 2(224 + T) + 3T.\n\nCompute 2*(224 + T) = 448 + 2T.\n\nSo,\n\n229 - z = 1124 - 448 - 2T + 3T.\n\nCompute 1124 - 448 = 676.\n\nThus,\n\n229 - z = 676 + T.\n\nTherefore,\n\n229 - z = 676 + T.\n\nSo, rearranged:\n\n-z = 676 + T - 229.\n\nCompute 676 - 229 = 447.\n\nThus,\n\n-z = 447 + T.\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\nz = -447 - T.\n\nBut z is the number of residents owning all four, so z must be positive. Also, T is the number of residents owning all three, which is positive.\n\nBut according to this, z = -447 - T, which is negative since T is positive. That can't be, as the number of residents can't be negative.\n\nThis suggests a mistake in my reasoning.\n\nWait, let me go back.\n\nSo, perhaps my expression for y is incorrect.\n\nEarlier, I had:\n\ny = |A| + |B| + |C| - 2(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut wait, no, actually, the correct formula for exactly one is:\n\nC(1) = |A| + |B| + |C| - 3|A \u2229 B| - 3|A \u2229 C| - 3|B \u2229 C| + 6|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, no, that seems too high.\n\nWait, let me think again.\n\nThe inclusion-exclusion principle for exactly one is:\n\nC(1) = |A| + |B| + |C| - 3|A \u2229 B| - 3|A \u2229 C| - 3|B \u2229 C| + 6|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut actually, no.\n\nWait, no, actually, the number of elements in exactly one set is:\n\nC(1) = |A| + |B| + |C| - 3|A \u2229 B| - 3|A \u2229 C| - 3|B \u2229 C| + 6|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, but I'm not sure. Maybe I should refer to the standard inclusion-exclusion formula.\n\nWait, actually, the standard inclusion-exclusion gives the total in at least one set as:\n\n|A \u222a B \u222a C| = |A| + |B| + |C| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut the number of elements in exactly one set is |A \u222a B \u222a C| minus those in exactly two or three sets.\n\nSo, exactly one = |A \u222a B \u222a C| - 2|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|).\n\nWait, maybe not. Let me think.\n\nWait, actually, the number of elements in exactly one set is:\n\n(|A| + |B| + |C| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 C|) - 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nNo, perhaps not. Let me try to think of it as:\n\nTotal in at least one set: |A \u222a B \u222a C| = |A| + |B| + |C| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nNumber in exactly one set = |A \u222a B \u222a C| - 2|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| - (|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|).\n\nWait, that seems too convoluted. Maybe it's better to express the number in exactly one set as:\n\n(|A| + |B| + |C|) - 2(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) - 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, no, that doesn't make sense.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to use the formula that exactly one = |A| + |B| + |C| - 2(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, let me test it with a simple case.\n\nSuppose |A| = 2, |B| = 2, |C| = 2.\n\n|A \u2229 B| = 1, |A \u2229 C| = 1, |B \u2229 C| = 1.\n\n|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = 1.\n\nThen, number in exactly one is:\n\nEach set has 2, overlapping pairwise with 1 each, and all three overlapping at 1.\n\nSo, the Venn diagram has:\n\n- Only A: 1\n\n- Only B: 1\n\n- Only C: 1\n\n- All three: 1.\n\nSo, total exactly one is 3.\n\nUsing the formula:\n\n|A| + |B| + |C| = 6\n\n- 2(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) = 2*(3) = 6\n\n+ 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = 3*1=3\n\nSo, 6 - 6 + 3 = 3, which matches.\n\nSo, yes, the formula seems to be:\n\nExactly one = |A| + |B| + |C| - 2(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nTherefore, in our case:\n\ny = |A| + |B| + |C| - 2(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWe have:\n\ny = 195 + 367 + 562 - 2(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nCompute 195 + 367 = 562, 562 + 562 = 1124.\n\nSo, y = 1124 - 2(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut from equation (1):\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) = 224 + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nSo, plug that into y:\n\ny = 1124 - 2(224 + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nCompute 2*224 = 448.\n\nThus,\n\ny = 1124 - 448 - 2|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nSimplify:\n\n1124 - 448 = 676.\n\nSo,\n\ny = 676 + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut we also have that y = 229 - z.\n\nFrom earlier, y = 229 - z.\n\nTherefore,\n\n676 + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C| = 229 - z.\n\nBut z is the number of residents who own all four, which is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, no. Wait, in the problem, it's stated that each resident owns a bag of candy hearts. So, the four things are:\n\n- Diamond ring (A)\n\n- Set of golf clubs (B)\n\n- Garden spade (C)\n\n- Candy hearts (D), which everyone has.\n\nBut the problem says \"each of the 900 residents owns a bag of candy hearts,\" so candy hearts are universal. So, when we talk about owning all four, it's owning A, B, C, and D.\n\nBut since D is owned by everyone, the number of residents owning all four is the same as the number of residents owning A, B, and C, which is |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nTherefore, z = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nSo, going back:\n\ny = 676 + z.\n\nBut y = 229 - z.\n\nTherefore,\n\n676 + z = 229 - z.\n\nBring z to left and constants to right:\n\n676 + z + z = 229\n\n676 + 2z = 229\n\n2z = 229 - 676\n\n2z = -447\n\nz = -447 / 2\n\nWait, that can't be, since z is a positive number of residents.\n\nHmm, this is a problem. So, something is wrong here.\n\nWait, let me retrace.\n\nWe have:\n\nTotal residents: 900.\n\nEach owns candy hearts, so all residents own D.\n\nNumber owning exactly two of A, B, C: 437.\n\nNumber owning exactly three of A, B, C: 234.\n\nSo, number owning exactly one of A, B, C is y = 900 - (437 + 234 + z).\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, the total number of residents is 900.\n\nEach resident owns D, so the breakdown is:\n\n- Own exactly one of A, B, C: y\n\n- Own exactly two of A, B, C: 437\n\n- Own exactly three of A, B, C: 234\n\n- Own all three A, B, C: z\n\nSo, total residents: y + 437 + 234 + z = 900.\n\nTherefore, y + z = 900 - 437 - 234 = 900 - 671 = 229.\n\nThus, y = 229 - z.\n\nEarlier, I had the same expression.\n\nThen, using inclusion-exclusion, I have:\n\ny = |A| + |B| + |C| - 2(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut |A| = 195, |B| = 367, |C| = 562.\n\nSo, 195 + 367 + 562 = 1124.\n\nThen,\n\ny = 1124 - 2(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nFrom equation (1):\n\n(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) = 224 + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nTherefore, substitute:\n\ny = 1124 - 2*(224 + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nCompute 2*224 = 448.\n\nThus,\n\ny = 1124 - 448 - 2|A \u2229 B \u2229 C| + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nSimplify:\n\n1124 - 448 = 676.\n\nSo,\n\ny = 676 + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut y = 229 - z.\n\nAnd z = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nThus,\n\n676 + z = 229 - z.\n\nWhich gives,\n\n676 + z + z = 229\n\n676 + 2z = 229\n\n2z = 229 - 676\n\n2z = -447\n\nz = -447 / 2\n\nWhich is negative, which is impossible.\n\nSo, that suggests that there is a contradiction, which can't be.\n\nTherefore, my initial assumption must be wrong.\n\nWait, perhaps I misapplied the inclusion-exclusion principle.\n\nWait, perhaps the number of residents owning exactly two is 437, exactly three is 234, exactly one is y, and exactly four is z.\n\nBut since everyone owns D, the four things are A, B, C, D.\n\nBut in the problem statement, it says: \"each of the 900 residents owns a bag of candy hearts.\" So, everyone owns D.\n\nTherefore, when they say \"own exactly two of these things,\" they mean exactly two among A, B, C, and D.\n\nBut since everyone owns D, the number of residents owning exactly two is those who own exactly two of A, B, C, and D.\n\nBut since D is owned by everyone, to have exactly two, they must have exactly one of A, B, C and D.\n\nWait, no. Wait, hold on.\n\nWait, if someone owns exactly two of t