one of A, B, C.\n\nBut how is that counted?\n\nAlternatively, the number of residents owning exactly one of A, B, C is equal to the number of residents owning exactly two (437) minus the number owning exactly three (234).\n\nWait, that might not be correct.\n\nWait, actually, the number of residents owning exactly one of A, B, C is equal to the number of residents who own exactly one of A, B, C and D.\n\nBut since D is everyone, that's equal to the number of residents owning exactly one of A, B, C.\n\nBut how is that related to the inclusion-exclusion counts?\n\nWait, perhaps we can use the formula:\n\nNumber of residents owning exactly one = |A| + |B| + |C| - 2(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, that seems complicated.\n\nAlternatively, let me think in terms of Venn diagrams.\n\nThe number of residents owning exactly one is:\n\n(|A| + |B| + |C|) - 3(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 6|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nWait, I'm not sure.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to refer to the standard formula for exactly one.\n\nIn inclusion-exclusion, the number of elements in exactly one of the sets is:\n\n|A \u222a B \u222a C| - 2(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut |A \u222a B \u222a C| is 900.\n\nSo,\n\nNumber of residents owning exactly one = 900 - 2(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut we also know that:\n\nNumber of residents owning exactly one = ?\n\nWait, actually, no, because the residents owning exactly one are those in exactly one of A, B, C, which is a separate count.\n\nBut since D is everyone, they all own D, so they are part of the four categories: exactly one, exactly two, exactly three, exactly four.\n\nBut since D is everyone, the counts are:\n\nExactly one: own exactly one of A, B, C and D.\n\nExactly two: own exactly two of A, B, C and D.\n\nExactly three: own exactly three of A, B, C and D.\n\nExactly four: own all four.\n\nSo, the number of residents owning exactly one is equal to the number of residents owning exactly one of A, B, C.\n\nSimilarly, exactly two is equal to exactly two of A, B, C.\n\nSimilarly, exactly three.\n\nSo, the number of residents owning exactly one is equal to the number of residents owning exactly one of A, B, C.\n\nWhich can be calculated as:\n\n(|A| + |B| + |C| - |A \u2229 B| - |A \u2229 C| - |B \u2229 C| + |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|) - 2(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C| - 2|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|).\n\nWait, maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, the number of residents owning exactly one is:\n\n(|A| + |B| + |C|) - 3(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut I'm not sure.\n\nWait, maybe I should refer back to the formula.\n\nIn inclusion-exclusion, the number of elements in exactly one of the sets is:\n\n|A| + |B| + |C| - 3(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut I'm not entirely sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to use the formula for exactly one:\n\nExactly one = (|A| + |B| + |C|) - 3(|A \u2229 B| + |A \u2229 C| + |B \u2229 C|) + 3|A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut this is getting too convoluted.\n\nWait, maybe let's think in terms of variables.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet a = number of residents owning only A.\n\nb = only B.\n\nc = only C.\n\nd = only A and B.\n\ne = only A and C.\n\nf = only B and C.\n\ng = all three A, B, C.\n\nh = all four A, B, C, D.\n\nSo, total residents:\n\na + b + c + d + e + f + g + h = 900.\n\nGiven:\n\nNumber owning exactly two: d + e + f = 437.\n\nNumber owning exactly three: g = 234.\n\nNumber owning all four: h = ?\n\nWe need to find h.\n\nAlso, the total number of residents owning A: a + d + e + g + h = 195.\n\nSimilarly, total owning B: b + d + f + g + h = 367.\n\nTotal owning C: c + e + f + g + h = 562.\n\nAlso, total owning D: everyone, so no constraint there.\n\nSo, we have:\n\n1. a + b + c + d + e + f + g + h = 900.\n\n2. d + e + f = 437.\n\n3. g = 234.\n\n4. a + d + e + g + h = 195.\n\n5. b + d + f + g + h = 367.\n\n6. c + e + f + g + h = 562.\n\nWe need to find h.\n\nLet me try to express variables in terms of others.\n\nFrom equation 2: d + e + f = 437.\n\nFrom equation 3: g = 234.\n\nFrom equation 4: a + d + e + 234 + h = 195 => a + d + e + h = 195 - 234 = -39.\n\nWait, that can't be. Negative number? That suggests a problem.\n\nWait, 195 - 234 = -39.\n\nThis is impossible because the number of residents can't be negative.\n\nSo, perhaps I made a mistake in the equations.\n\nWait, equation 4: a + d + e + g + h = 195.\n\nBut g = 234, so substituting:\n\na + d + e + 234 + h = 195.\n\nTherefore, a + d + e + h = 195 - 234 = -39.\n\nNegative, which is impossible. So, this suggests that there is an inconsistency in the problem or my setup is wrong.\n\nWait, maybe I misapplied the equations.\n\nWait, let's check:\n\nTotal owning A: a + (d + e + g) + h = 195.\n\nSimilarly, total owning B: b + (d + f + g) + h = 367.\n\nTotal owning C: c + (e + f + g) + h = 562.\n\nTotal owning exactly two: d + e + f = 437.\n\nTotal owning exactly three: g = 234.\n\nTotal owning all four: h.\n\nSo, from total owning A:\n\na + (d + e + g) + h = 195.\n\nBut d + e + f = 437, so d + e = 437 - f.\n\nThus,\n\na + (437 - f) + g + h = 195.\n\nSimilarly, g = 234, so:\n\na + 437 - f + 234 + h = 195.\n\nThus,\n\na - f + h = 195 - 437 - 234 = 195 - 671 = -476.\n\nSo,\n\na - f + h = -476.\n\nSimilarly, from total owning B:\n\nb + (d + f + g) + h = 367.\n\nAgain, d + f + g = (d + f) + g.\n\nFrom d + e + f = 437, so d + f = 437 - e.\n\nThus,\n\nb + (437 - e) + g + h = 367.\n\nSubstituting g = 234,\n\nb + 437 - e + 234 + h = 367.\n\nThus,\n\nb - e + h = 367 - 437 - 234 = 367 - 671 = -304.\n\nSo,\n\nb - e + h = -304.\n\nFrom total owning C:\n\nc + (e + f + g) + h = 562.\n\nAgain, e + f + g = (e + f) + g.\n\nFrom d + e + f = 437, e + f = 437 - d.\n\nThus,\n\nc + (437 - d) + g + h = 562.\n\nSubstituting g = 234,\n\nc + 437 - d + 234 + h = 562.\n\nThus,\n\nc - d + h = 562 - 437 - 234 = 562 - 671 = -109.\n\nSo,\n\nc - d + h = -109.\n\nNow, let me summarize the equations:\n\nFrom A:\n\na - f + h = -476. (Equation A)\n\nFrom B:\n\nb - e + h = -304. (Equation B)\n\nFrom C:\n\nc - d + h = -109. (Equation C)\n\nWe also have:\n\nFrom exactly two: d + e + f = 437. (Equation D)\n\nFrom exactly three: g = 234. (Equation E)\n\nWe also have the total residents:\n\na + b + c + d + e + f + g + h = 900.\n\nSubstituting Equations D and E:\n\na + b + c + 437 + 234 + h = 900.\n\nSo,\n\na + b + c + h = 900 - 437 - 234 = 900 - 671 = 229.\n\nSo,\n\na + b + c + h = 229. (Equation F)\n\nSo, now we have:\n\nEquation A: a - f + h = -476.\n\nEquation B: b - e + h = -304.\n\nEquation C: c - d + h = -109.\n\nEquation D: d + e + f = 437.\n\nEquation F: a + b + c + h = 229.\n\nWe need to solve for h.\n\nLet me see if I can express a, b, c in terms of f, e, d, etc.\n\nFrom Equation A: a = -476 + f - h.\n\nFrom Equation B: b = -304 + e - h.\n\nFrom Equation C: c = -109 + d - h.\n\nSubstitute a, b, c into Equation F:\n\n(-476 + f - h) + (-304 + e - h) + (-109 + d - h) + h = 229.\n\nSimplify:\n\n-476 - 304 - 109 + f + e + d - h - h - h + h = 229.\n\nCompute constants: -476 - 304 = -780; -780 - 109 = -889.\n\nVariables: f + e + d - 3h + h = f + e + d - 2h.\n\nThus,\n\n-889 + f + e + d - 2h = 229.\n\nBring -889 to the other side:\n\nf + e + d - 2h = 229 + 889 = 1118.\n\nBut from Equation D: d + e + f = 437.\n\nSo,\n\n437 - 2h = 1118.\n\nThus,\n\n-2h = 1118 - 437 = 681.\n\nSo,\n\nh = -681 / 2 = -340.5.\n\nWait, that can't be. h is the number of residents, which can't be negative or a fraction.\n\nThis suggests something is wrong. Maybe I made a mistake in the algebra.\n\nLet me check the substitution step.\n\nFrom Equation A: a = -476 + f - h.\n\nEquation B: b = -304 + e - h.\n\nEquation C: c = -109 + d - h.\n\nSubstitute into Equation F:\n\na + b + c + h = (-476 + f - h) + (-304 + e - h) + (-109 + d - h) + h = 229.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n-476 + f - h -304 + e - h -109 + d - h + h.\n\nCombine constants: -476 - 304 - 109 = -889.\n\nVariables: f + e + d.\n\nVariables with h: -h - h - h + h = -2h.\n\nThus,\n\n-889 + f + e + d - 2h = 229.\n\nBut f + e + d = 437 (from Equation D), so:\n\n-889 + 437 - 2h = 229.\n\nCompute: -889 + 437 = -452.\n\nThus,\n\n-452 - 2h = 229.\n\nSo,\n\n-2h = 229 + 452 = 681.\n\nThus,\n\nh = 681 / (-2) = -340.5.\n\nStill negative. Hmm.\n\nThis suggests an inconsistency in the problem.\n\nBut the problem statement says 900 residents, each owning a bag of candy hearts, so the counts of owning other things must be subsets.\n\nWait, perhaps the numbers are inconsistent? Because getting h negative suggests something is wrong.\n\nBut the problem says \"437 residents who own exactly two of these things, and 234 residents who own exactly three of these things.\" So, if all residents own a bag of candy hearts, then the rest of the things (A, B, C) are additional to that.\n\nWait, perhaps I misunderstood the problem. Maybe \"exactly two of these things\" includes the candy hearts? But the problem says \"each of the 900 residents owns a bag of candy hearts.\" So, the candy hearts are separate.\n\nWait, let me read the problem again.\n\n\"Among the 900 residents of Aimeville, there are 195 who own a diamond ring, 367 who own a set of golf clubs, and 562 who own a garden spade. In addition, each of the 900 residents owns a bag of candy hearts. There are 437 residents who own exactly two of these things, and 234 residents who own exactly three of these things. Find the number of residents of Aimeville who own all four of these things.\"\n\nSo, the four things are diamond ring, golf clubs, garden spade, and candy hearts.\n\nSo, when it says \"there are 437 residents who own exactly two of these things,\" it means exactly two of the four: diamond ring, golf clubs, garden spade, and candy hearts.\n\nSimilarly, 234 residents own exactly three of the four.\n\nBut since all residents own candy hearts, the only way to own exactly two is to own candy hearts plus one of the other three.\n\nSimilarly, exactly three is candy hearts plus two of the other three.\n\nAnd exactly four is candy hearts plus all three.\n\nSo, let's denote:\n\nLet me define variables:\n\nLet x = number of residents who own exactly two things: candy hearts + one other.\n\nLet y = number of residents who own exactly three things: candy hearts + two others.\n\nLet z = number of residents who own all four: candy hearts + all three others.\n\nSo, total residents: x + y + z = 900.\n\nGiven:\n\nx = 437 (exactly two: candy hearts + one other).\n\ny = 234 (exactly three: candy hearts + two others).\n\nz = ? (exactly four: candy hearts + all three others).\n\nWe need to find z.\n\nAlso, we have the counts for each thing:\n\nDiamond rings: 195.\n\nGolf clubs: 367.\n\nGarden spade: 562.\n\nCandy hearts: 900.\n\nSo, for diamond rings: some own only diamond rings, some own diamond rings and others.\n\nSimilarly for golf clubs and garden spades.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nOnly diamond rings: a.\n\nOnly golf clubs: b.\n\nOnly garden spades: c.\n\nDiamond rings and golf clubs: d.\n\nDiamond rings and garden spades: e.\n\nGolf clubs and garden spades: f.\n\nAll three: g.\n\nAll four: z.\n\nSo, the total ownership:\n\nDiamond rings: a + d + e + g + z = 195.\n\nGolf clubs: b + d + f + g + z = 367.\n\nGarden spades: c + e + f + g + z = 562.\n\nCandy hearts: everyone owns it, so no one owns just candy hearts.\n\nNow, the residents who own exactly two things: x = d + e + f.\n\nGiven x = 437.\n\nResidents who own exactly three things: y = g + z.\n\nGiven y = 234.\n\nResidents who own exactly four: z.\n\nSo, total residents:\n\nx + y + z = 437 + 234 + z = 671 + z = 900.\n\nThus, z = 900 - 671 = 229.\n\nWait, that seems straightforward.\n\nWait, let me check.\n\nTotal residents: x + y + z = 437 + 234 + z = 671 + z = 900 => z = 229.\n\nSo, the number of residents who own all four is 229.\n\nBut let me verify with the other equations.\n\nDiamond rings: a + d + e + g + z = 195.\n\nBut a + d + e + g + z = (a + d + e + g) + z.\n\nBut a + d + e + g = (only diamond rings + those with diamond rings and others).\n\nBut since x = d + e + f = 437.\n\nSo, d + e + f = 437.\n\nThus, d + e = 437 - f.\n\nBut not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe not necessary.\n\nWait, maybe I can use the counts for each thing.\n\nDiamond rings: a + d + e + g + z = 195.\n\nGolf clubs: b + d + f + g + z = 367.\n\nGarden spades: c + e + f + g + z = 562.\n\nWe can write:\n\nDiamond rings: a + d + e + g + z = 195. (1)\n\nGolf clubs: b + d + f + g + z = 367. (2)\n\nGarden spades: c + e + f + g + z = 562. (3)\n\nWe also have:\n\nOnly candy hearts: 0 (since everyone owns candy hearts and something else).\n\nTotal residents: a + b + c + d + e + f + g + z = 900.\n\nBut since x = d + e + f = 437, y = g + z = 234, so:\n\na + b + c + d + e + f + g + z = a + b + c + (d + e + f) + (g + z) = a + b + c + 437 + 234 = a + b + c + 671 = 900.\n\nThus, a + b + c = 900 - 671 = 229. (4)\n\nNow, from equations (1), (2), (3):\n\nFrom (1): a + d + e + g + z = 195.\n\nFrom (2): b + d + f + g + z = 367.\n\nFrom (3): c + e + f + g + z = 562.\n\nWe can write:\n\nFrom (1): a = 195 - d - e - g - z.\n\nFrom (2): b = 367 - d - f - g - z.\n\nFrom (3): c = 562 - e - f - g - z.\n\nSubstitute into (4):\n\na + b + c = (195 - d - e - g - z) + (367 - d - f - g - z) + (562 - e - f - g - z) = 229.\n\nCompute:\n\n195 + 367 + 562 - (d + e + g + z) - (d + f + g + z) - (e + f + g + z) = 229.\n\nWait, no, more carefully:\n\nEach term is subtracted:\n\n= 195 - d - e - g - z + 367 - d - f - g - z + 562 - e - f - g - z.\n\nSo, constants: 195 + 367 + 562 = 1124.\n\nVariables: -d - e - g - z - d - f - g - z - e - f - g - z.\n\nSo, total variables subtracted:\n\n-2d - 2e - 2f - 3g - 3z.\n\nThus,\n\n1124 - 2d - 2e - 2f - 3g - 3z = 229.\n\nSo,\n\n-2d - 2e - 2f - 3g - 3z = 229 - 1124 = -895.\n\nMultiply both sides by (-1):\n\n2d + 2e + 2f + 3g + 3z = 895.\n\nBut from x = d + e + f = 437.\n\nThus, d + e + f = 437.\n\nSo, 2d + 2e + 2f = 2*437 = 874.\n\nThus,\n\n874 + 3g + 3z = 895.\n\nSo,\n\n3g + 3z = 895 - 874 = 21.\n\nDivide both sides by 3:\n\ng + z = 7.\n\nBut y = g + z = 234.\n\nWait, no, y is the number of residents who own exactly three things, which is g + z.\n\nWait, hold on. Earlier, I thought y = g + z, but in reality, y is the number of residents who own exactly three things: which would be g + z.\n\nWait, but the problem says \"234 residents who own exactly three of these things.\"\n\nSo, y = g + z = 234.\n\nBut according to the above, 3g + 3z = 21, so g + z = 7.\n\nBut that's a conflict because g + z should be 234.\n\nThis is a contradiction.\n\nHmm, this suggests that either the problem is inconsistent or my approach is wrong.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a miscalculation.\n\nLet me check:\n\nFrom (1): a + d + e + g + z = 195.\n\nFrom (2): b + d + f + g + z = 367.\n\nFrom (3): c + e + f + g + z = 562.\n\nFrom x: d + e + f = 437.\n\nFrom y: g + z = 234.\n\nFrom total: a + b + c + d + e + f + g + z = 900.\n\nWait, let me try substituting.\n\nFrom (1): a = 195 - d - e - g - z.\n\nFrom (2): b = 367 - d - f - g - z.\n\nFrom (3): c = 562 - e - f - g - z.\n\nFrom x: f = 437 - d - e.\n\nFrom y: g + z = 234, so g = 234 - z.\n\nSo, plug f = 437 - d - e into b and c:\n\nb = 367 - d - (437 - d - e) - g - z = 367 - d -437 + d + e - g - z = (367 - 437) + e - g - z = (-70) + e - g - z.\n\nBut g + z = 234, so -g - z = -234.\n\nThus, b = -70 + e -234 = e - 304.\n\nSimilarly, c = 562 - e - (437 - d - e) - g - z = 562 - e -437 + d + e - g - z = (562 -437) + d - g - z = 125 + d - g - z.\n\nBut g + z =234, so -g - z = -234.\n\nThus, c = 125 + d -234 = d -109.\n\nNow, total residents:\n\na + b + c + d + e + f + g + z = (195 - d - e - g - z) + (e - 304) + (d -109) + d + e + (437 - d - e) + g + z.\n\nSimplify:\n\n195 - d - e - g - z + e - 304 + d -109 + d + e +437 - d - e + g + z.\n\nNow, let's cancel terms:\n\n- d and + d: cancels.\n\n- e and + e: cancels.\n\n- g and + g: cancels.\n\n- z and + z: cancels.\n\nSo, remaining:\n\n195 -304 -109 +437.\n\nCompute:\n\n195 -304 = -109.\n\n-109 -109 = -218.\n\n-218 +437 = 219.\n\nBut total residents should be 900, so 219 \u2260 900.\n\nThis is a contradiction.\n\nWait, so there's a problem here. Maybe the numbers given in the problem are inconsistent?\n\nAlternatively, maybe my approach is wrong.\n\nWait, maybe I misapplied the inclusion-exclusion principle.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I need to use the principle of inclusion-exclusion for four sets, but it's getting complicated.\n\nWait, maybe I can think of it as a Venn diagram with four sets: A, B, C, D. But D is everyone, so it's redundant.\n\nWait, since D is everyone, then the regions outside D don't exist. So, the Venn diagram effectively has A, B, C, and D, but D covers all.\n\nBut then, the counts for A, B, C are subsets of D.\n\nSo, the number of residents who own all four is z.\n\nSo, z is the number of residents who own A, B, C, and D.\n\nBut since D is everyone, it's the intersection of A, B, C with D, which is just A \u2229 B \u2229 C.\n\nSo, z = |A \u2229 B \u2229 C|.\n\nBut how does this help?\n\nWait, perhaps we need to use inclusion-exclusion on A, B, C.\n\nThe total number of residents is 900.\n\nThe number of residents who own exactly two of A, B, C is 437.\n\nThe number who own exactly three is 234.\n\nSo, the number of residents who own exactly two or three is 437 + 234 = 671.\n\nTherefore, the number of residents who own at most one of A, B, C is 900 - 671 = 229.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet a = number who own only A.\n\nb = only B.\n\nc = only C.\n\nd = only A and B.\n\ne = only A and C.\n\nf = only B and C.\n\ng = A, B, and C.\n\nh = all four, but since D is everyone, h = z.\n\nSo, a + b + c + d + e + f + g + h = 229.\n\nBut we also have:\n\nd + e + f + g = 437 (exactly two).\n\ng + h = 234 (exactly three).\n\nBut h = z.\n\nWait, but h is also part of the exactly three, so h is the number who own A, B, C, and D, whi